---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const description =
  'A plain-language explanation of Paroxysmal Nocturnal Haemoglobinuria (PNH): what causes it, its symptoms, how it is diagnosed with flow cytometry, and how it is treated.';
---

<Base title="What is PNH?" description={description}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Understanding the condition"
    title="What is PNH?"
    lede="Paroxysmal Nocturnal Haemoglobinuria is a rare, acquired blood disorder. This page explains it in ordinary language — what goes wrong, what it feels like, how it is confirmed, and what can be done about it."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <div class="disclaimer" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
          <p>
            <strong>This is general information, not medical advice.</strong>
            PNH affects different people very differently. Nothing here should
            be used to make decisions about your own care — bring your questions
            to a haematologist who knows your case.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>The name, unpacked</h2>
        <p>
          The name is a mouthful, and it is also slightly misleading — which is
          worth knowing, because it confuses a lot of people early on.
        </p>
        <dl class="definition-list">
          <dt>Paroxysmal</dt>
          <dd>
            "Sudden and irregular." Symptoms were originally thought to come in
            unpredictable attacks. In reality, the underlying process is usually
            continuous, with flare-ups on top.
          </dd>
          <dt>Nocturnal</dt>
          <dd>
            "At night." Named because patients often noticed dark urine first
            thing in the morning. That is mostly because urine is concentrated
            overnight — the destruction of red cells happens around the clock.
          </dd>
          <dt>Haemoglobinuria</dt>
          <dd>
            Haemoglobin — the oxygen-carrying pigment released when red blood
            cells break apart — appearing in the urine, turning it tea-,
            cola- or red-coloured.
          </dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
          So: a condition in which red blood cells break down, releasing
          haemoglobin into the urine. Importantly, not every person with PNH
          ever sees dark urine. Its absence does not rule PNH out.
        </p>

        <h2>What actually goes wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Blood cells are made by stem cells in the bone marrow. In PNH, one of
          those stem cells acquires a change in a gene called <em>PIGA</em>.
          This is a <strong>somatic</strong> mutation — it happens in a single
          cell during a person's life. It is not inherited from your parents and
          you cannot pass it to your children.
        </p>
        <p>
          That faulty stem cell copies itself, producing a population of blood
          cells missing a molecular "anchor" that normally holds protective
          proteins onto the cell surface. Two of those missing proteins,
          <strong>CD55</strong> and <strong>CD59</strong>, are the important
          ones. Their job is to hold back the <strong>complement system</strong>
          — an arm of the immune system that destroys bacteria and damaged
          cells.
        </p>
        <p>
          Without that brake, complement attacks the person's own red blood
          cells and bursts them. This is called <strong>intravascular
          haemolysis</strong>, and nearly everything else about PNH follows from
          it.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout--brand">
          <p>
            A useful way to picture it: the complement system is a security
            system that destroys anything without the right ID badge. In PNH,
            some of your own red blood cells are made without a badge. The
            security system is working exactly as designed — it just cannot
            tell that these cells belong to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Symptoms</h2>
        <p>
          PNH varies enormously between individuals. Some people have mild
          disease found incidentally; others are seriously unwell. Common
          features include:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Fatigue</strong> — usually the dominant symptom, and often
            out of proportion to the degree of anaemia. Many patients describe
            it as the hardest part of the condition to live with, and the hardest
            to explain to other people.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Anaemia</strong> — breathlessness, pallor, palpitations,
            dizziness, poor concentration.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Dark urine</strong> — most noticeable in the morning, and
            not present in everyone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Blood clots (thrombosis)</strong> — the most serious
            complication of PNH, and historically its leading cause of death.
            Clots can form in unusual places, including veins in the abdomen and
            the brain. Sudden severe abdominal pain, a swollen painful leg,
            chest pain or breathlessness need <strong>urgent</strong> medical
            attention.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Abdominal pain, difficulty swallowing, and erectile
            dysfunction</strong> — these are caused by smooth muscle spasm, a
            knock-on effect of free haemoglobin mopping up nitric oxide in the
            blood. They are genuine PNH symptoms and worth raising with your
            doctor even though they can be embarrassing to bring up.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Kidney problems</strong> and <strong>raised pressure in the
            lungs</strong> can develop over time with sustained haemolysis.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>PNH, aplastic anaemia and bone marrow failure</h2>
        <p>
          PNH is closely linked to <strong>aplastic anaemia</strong>, a
          condition in which the bone marrow fails to produce enough blood
          cells. A significant proportion of people with aplastic anaemia carry
          a PNH clone, and some people with PNH go on to develop marrow failure.
          Because of this, anyone diagnosed with aplastic anaemia or an
          unexplained low blood count should be tested for PNH — and people with
          PNH are usually monitored for marrow problems.
        </p>

        <h2>Why diagnosis is so often delayed</h2>
        <p>
          Delays of months or years between first symptoms and a confirmed
          diagnosis are common, and this is one of the main things we work to
          change. The reasons are structural, not anyone's fault:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>PNH is rare enough that most clinicians never encounter a case.</li>
          <li>
            Its symptoms — tiredness, anaemia, abdominal pain — are extremely
            common and point to many other things first.
          </li>
          <li>
            Patients are often treated for iron-deficiency anaemia for a long
            time before anyone asks why the anaemia is not responding.
          </li>
          <li>
            The confirmatory test is not part of a routine workup; someone has
            to specifically think of PNH and order it.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you have unexplained haemolytic anaemia, an unexplained blood clot
          in an unusual site, or persistently low blood counts, it is reasonable
          to ask your doctor directly: <em>"Should I be tested for PNH?"</em>
        </p>

        <h2>How PNH is diagnosed</h2>
        <p>
          The definitive test is <strong>high-sensitivity flow cytometry</strong>
          on a sample of peripheral blood. The laboratory examines the surface of
          your blood cells and measures what proportion are missing CD55 and
          CD59. Testing usually includes a reagent called <abbr title="Fluorescently labelled aerolysin">FLAER</abbr>,
          which improves accuracy on white blood cells.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is reported as the size of your <strong>PNH clone</strong>
          — the percentage of your blood cells affected. That number matters: it
          influences whether you need treatment and how closely you are
          monitored, and it is usually rechecked over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two practical points patients often ask about:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>It is a blood test, not a bone marrow biopsy.</strong> A
            marrow test may be done separately to look for aplastic anaemia or
            other marrow conditions, but it is not how PNH itself is confirmed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Older tests are obsolete.</strong> The Ham test and sucrose
            lysis test have been replaced by flow cytometry, which is far more
            sensitive.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How PNH is treated</h2>
        <p>
          Treatment depends on the size of the clone, how much haemolysis is
          happening, symptoms, and whether there is bone marrow failure
          alongside. Not everyone with a PNH clone needs drug treatment — some
          people are monitored rather than treated.
        </p>

        <h3>Complement inhibitors</h3>
        <p>
          These are the disease-modifying treatments, and they changed the
          outlook for PNH fundamentally. They work by blocking the complement
          system at various points so it can no longer destroy the unprotected
          red cells. Several classes now exist:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>C5 inhibitors</strong> (eculizumab, ravulizumab, crovalimab)
            — the longest-established approach, given by infusion or injection.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>C3 inhibitors</strong> (pegcetacoplan) and
            <strong>Factor B / Factor D inhibitors</strong> (iptacopan,
            danicopan) — newer options that act earlier in the complement
            pathway. Some are taken orally.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>An important safety point</h3>
          <p>
            Blocking complement raises the risk of serious infection,
            particularly <strong>meningococcal disease</strong>. Anyone starting
            a complement inhibitor must be vaccinated against meningococcus
            beforehand, may be given preventive antibiotics, and should be given
            a patient alert card to carry. If you are on one of these drugs and
            develop a fever, treat it as urgent and seek medical help
            immediately — do not wait to see if it settles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Supportive care</h3>
        <p>
          Alongside or instead of complement inhibitors, care may include blood
          transfusions, folic acid and iron supplementation, and anticoagulation
          to prevent or treat clots. This kind of support matters a great deal
          for quality of life.
        </p>

        <h3>Bone marrow transplant</h3>
        <p>
          An allogeneic stem cell transplant is the only treatment that can cure
          PNH, but it carries substantial risks of its own. It is generally
          reserved for people with severe bone marrow failure, or where other
          treatment is not an option. It is a decision to work through carefully
          with a transplant centre.
        </p>

        <div class="todo-note">
          TODO (Foundation): add an India-specific section here covering — which
